A||N||6||Dan87GT||Rear inner tie rod ends(good?)||||1||
Z||000000||Dan87GT||03-29-2000||03:06 PM||||Can someone tell me how to tell if the rear inner tie rods on my 87GT are bad. At $75 bucks apeice I would rather not replace them. The car has 104,000, and I replaced the balljoints, bushings, spring, struts, and outter tie rod ends.   hanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||03-29-2000||05:35 PM||||Jack up the rear of the car and crawl under the car until you can eyeball the tie rod real good. Have an assistant grab the rear tire at the 3 and 9 o'clock positions and try yanking on the tire back and forth. There should not be any play, if so it needs replaced.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||03-29-2000||09:01 PM||||For that price you might as well get the bump stop kit Bats got. It's cheaper.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodney||03-29-2000||10:38 PM||||I should be stocking some suspension parts soon. I hope I will be able to get the rear tie rods. I bet my price will be a lot less than $75 each if I can get them.<P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000004||Richard Parnell||03-30-2000||01:04 AM||||Go Rodney-  I'm in the market for a major portion of the suspension replacement parts on my car. Mybe you could find front end parts at a good price also -hint, hint.<BR>Got the dew tool, now I can work on my windows.  Thanks||||reg||10
Z||000005||Dan87GT||03-30-2000||07:33 AM||||Thanks guys, I have the tie rods off and replaced the outer tie rod ends, but how can you tell if the inner end is bad(end that bolts to the frame)||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||03-30-2000||11:47 AM||||There should be no play and it should move smoothly without catching. If held out vertically when it's off the car, it should not flop down just by gravity.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-30-2000).]||||reg||1
